9. Foreign Direct Investment: Determinants and Theories
Paper: International Economics (ECNCC301) | Unit: II | Topic: Reasons for international capital movement, FDI, determinants of FDI, theory of FDI
Introduction
FDI is investment by a resident of one country in an enterprise in another, giving the investor lasting interest and control (usually 10 per cent or more of voting power). It is the main channel of multinational activity and of technology transfer.
Reasons for international capital movement
Capital flows from where returns are low to where they are high, as the MacDougall model shows: marginal product of capital is higher in capital-scarce countries. Other motives are portfolio diversification, avoiding trade barriers, securing resources and markets, and exploiting a firm's technology.
Determinants of FDI
• Market size and growth of the host country.
• Cost factors: wages, productivity, infrastructure, resource availability.
• Policy: tax incentives, openness, ownership rules, investment protection.
• Macro stability: exchange rate, inflation, political risk.
• Institutions: rule of law, corruption, labour market flexibility.
• Agglomeration: presence of other firms and suppliers.
Theories of FDI
1. Capital-market / differential rates of return: capital moves to higher returns; fails to explain two-way FDI.
2. Hymer-Kindleberger monopolistic advantage theory: a firm needs a firm-specific advantage (technology, brand, management) to compensate for foreignness.
3. Product cycle theory (Vernon): firms shift production abroad as products mature.
4. Internalisation theory (Buckley-Casson, Coase): firms prefer FDI to licensing when markets for knowledge are imperfect, avoiding transaction costs.
5. Dunning's eclectic (OLI) paradigm: FDI happens when a firm has Ownership advantages, the host has Location advantages, and Internalisation is more profitable than market transactions.
6. Kojima's model: Japanese-style FDI is trade-creating, moving from the comparative-disadvantage industry of the home country.
Conclusion
No single theory explains FDI; the OLI framework integrates the main insights and remains the most widely used.